<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Schmalkaldic League</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1531">1531</a>–<a href="/years/1547">1547</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Schmalkaldic League was a defensive alliance of Lutheran princes and imperial cities of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, founded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkalden">Schmalkalden</a> in 1531 and led by Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> and John Frederick of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>. It resisted Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a>'s efforts to restore Catholic unity until its defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a>, at Mühlberg in 1547. Members such as Constance and Lingen were then punished with loss of status or land.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league.jpg" alt="Schmalkaldic League" width="320" height="253" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ernio48 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Schmalkaldischer_Bund.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The League was officially established on 27 February 1531 as a defensive religious alliance: its members pledged to defend each other if Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> attacked their territories. At the insistence of the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a>, membership was conditional on agreement to the Lutheran Augsburg Confession or the Reformed Tetrapolitan Confession. Unlike earlier alliances such as the League of Torgau it had a substantial military, the members agreeing to provide 10,000 infantry and 2,000 cavalry for their mutual protection.</p><p>Its formation, together with the advance of Sultan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suleiman-the-magnificent">Suleiman the Magnificent</a> in 1532, led Ferdinand of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> to grant a religious peace, concluded at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuremberg">Nuremberg</a> on 23 July 1532. Supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> obtained religious liberty until the meeting of a council.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In December 1535 the League opened itself to anyone who subscribed to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg-confession">Augsburg Confession</a>, and Anhalt, Württemberg, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pomerania">Pomerania</a> and the cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/augsburg">Augsburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kempten">Kempten</a> joined. It allied with Denmark-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> in 1538 and gained the Electoral Palatinate in 1545. The members rarely provoked Charles directly, but they confiscated church land, expelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> and Catholic princes and helped spread <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> through northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. Martin Luther attended the meeting of 1537, for which he prepared the Smalcald Articles; they were not formally adopted then but entered the Book of Concord in 1580.</p><p>For fifteen years the League met no opposition, because Charles was at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>. Francis I of France, though he persecuted Protestants at home, supported the princes from 1535 until the Treaty of Crépy in 1544. Once at peace, Charles turned on the League. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-war">Schmalkaldic War</a> of 1546–1547 its leaders could not agree on a plan of battle; on 24 April 1547 the imperial forces routed them at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-muhlberg">Battle of Mühlberg</a>, and Philip of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> surrendered in May. Reformers such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-bucer">Martin Bucer</a> fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In 1548 Charles forced the League to accept the Augsburg Interim. A Protestant victory in the Second Schmalkaldic War in 1552 made him flee across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alps">Alps</a>, and Ferdinand signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-passau">Peace of Passau</a>, which granted some freedoms to Protestants. Three years later the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peace-of-augsburg">Peace of Augsburg</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutheranism">Lutheranism</a> official status in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> and let princes choose the religion of their domains, by the principle of cuius regio, eius religio.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Schmalkaldic League” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Ernestine elector John Frederick I led the Protestant <b>Schmalkaldic League</b>, and his defeat and capture by Emperor Charles V at the Battle of Mühlberg in 1547 cost his line the electoral title.</blockquote><a href="/empire/electorate-of-saxony">Electorate of Saxony · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Charles V, long distracted by wars with France and the Ottoman Empire, defeated their <b>Schmalkaldic League</b> at the Battle of Mühlberg in 1547 but could not restore the old faith, and the Peace of Augsburg of 1555 allowed each prince of the empire to choose Catholicism or Lutheranism…</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>At Frankfurt-on-the-Main, where Philip Melanchthon represented the Lutherans, the emperor promised no violent action against the <b>Schmalkaldic League</b> for fifteen months from 1 May.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/april-19">19 April in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Count Konrad of Tecklenburg-Lingen joined the <b>Schmalkaldic League</b> and introduced Lutheranism; after its defeat Emperor Charles V confiscated Lingen in 1548 and gave it to Maximilian of Egmond-Buren, whose heiress married William of Orange.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-lingen">County of Lingen</a></li><li><blockquote>As a member of the defeated <b>Schmalkaldic League</b>, Constance was taken by Charles V in 1548 and given to the Austrian Habsburgs, losing its freedom and returning to Catholicism.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-constance">Free Imperial City of Constance</a></li><li><blockquote>Philip I the Magnanimous introduced the Reformation in 1526, founded the University of Marburg in 1527, hosted the Marburg Colloquy between Luther and Zwingli in 1529 and co-led the <b>Schmalkaldic League</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/landgraviate-of-hesse">Landgraviate of Hesse</a></li></ul></section>
